# 1. Distributed web infrastructure

A three-server web infrastructure hosting the website **www.foobar.com**.

## Diagram



![Distributed web infrastructure diagram](https://i.imgur.com/REPLACE_WITH_YOUR_IMAGE.png)

## Components

| Component | Description |
|-----------|-------------|
| Domain name | `foobar.com`, with a `www` A record pointing to `8.8.8.8` (the load balancer) |
| Load balancer | 1 server running HAproxy |
| Server 1 | Nginx (web server), application server, application files, MySQL (**Primary**) |
| Server 2 | Nginx (web server), application server, application files, MySQL (**Replica**) |

## What happens when a user visits www.foobar.com

1. The user types `www.foobar.com` in the browser.
2. The DNS answers with the IP of the `www` record: `8.8.8.8`, the load
   balancer.
3. The browser sends an HTTP/HTTPS request to the load balancer.
4. HAproxy chooses one of the two servers (Round Robin) and forwards the
   request to its Nginx.
5. Nginx serves static files directly, or forwards dynamic requests to the
   application server.
6. The application server runs the code and queries the database: writes go to
   the Primary, reads can go to the Primary or the Replica.
7. The response goes back through Nginx and the load balancer to the user.

## Specifics

### Why each additional element is added

- **Second server:** to add redundancy and capacity. If one server fails or is
  being maintained, the other can keep serving the website, and the traffic is
  shared between two servers instead of one.
- **Load balancer (HAproxy):** to distribute the incoming traffic between the
  servers, so that none of them is overloaded. It also checks the health of the
  servers and stops sending requests to one that is down, which improves
  availability and lets us add more servers later.
- **Database Primary-Replica cluster:** to have a live copy of the data on
  another server (protection against data loss), to spread the read requests
  between two databases, and to be able to promote the Replica if the Primary
  fails.

### Distribution algorithm of the load balancer
The load balancer is configured with **Round Robin**. It sends the requests to
the servers one after the other, in a fixed order: request 1 goes to Server 1,
request 2 to Server 2, request 3 to Server 1 again, and so on. Each server
receives about the same number of requests. (With the weighted version, servers
with more resources receive more requests.)

### Active-Active or Active-Passive
This load balancer enables an **Active-Active** setup: both servers are active
at the same time and both handle user traffic.

- **Active-Active:** all the nodes receive and process requests at the same
  time. The load is shared, so the capacity is higher, and if one node fails
  the other nodes keep serving.
- **Active-Passive:** only one node (the active one) handles the traffic. The
  other node (the passive one) is on standby and takes over only when the
  active node fails. Resources of the passive node are unused most of the time.

### How a database Primary-Replica (Master-Slave) cluster works
The **Primary** node is the main database: it accepts the write operations
(INSERT, UPDATE, DELETE) and records every change in a binary log. The
**Replica** node connects to the Primary, copies these changes and applies them
to its own data, so it always holds a copy of the Primary's data (with a small
delay). The Replica is used to answer read operations (SELECT), and as a backup
or a substitute if the Primary fails.

### Difference between the Primary node and the Replica node for the application
- The **Primary** accepts **writes** (and reads). The application must send all
  its write requests to the Primary.
- The **Replica** is **read-only** for the application. It can answer read
  requests, which reduces the load on the Primary. If the application wrote
  directly to the Replica, the data would be different from the Primary and
  would not be copied back.

## Issues with this infrastructure

### Where are the SPOF (Single Points Of Failure)?
- **The load balancer:** there is only one. If it fails, nobody can reach the
  website even though both servers are healthy.
- **The Primary database:** there is only one node accepting writes. If it
  fails, the application cannot write data until the Replica is promoted to
  Primary (and this is not automatic here).
- The DNS record also points to a single IP (the load balancer).

### Security issues
- **No firewall:** the servers and their services are exposed to the Internet
  and nothing filters the incoming traffic, so any open port can be attacked.
- **No HTTPS:** the traffic between the users and the website is not encrypted
  (no SSL/TLS certificate), so data such as passwords can be read or modified
  by someone intercepting it.

### No monitoring
Nothing watches the infrastructure. We have no data on the health of the
servers (CPU, memory, disk), on the number of requests, or on errors, and
nobody is alerted when a component fails or is overloaded, so problems are
discovered only when users complain.
